106.pdf?arnumber=1656106", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2017, 3, 15]], "date-time": "2017-03-15T13:25:04Z", "timestamp": 1489584304000}, "score": 29.54866, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/1656106/"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[null]]}, "references-count": 22, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1109/aero.2006.1656106"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2024, 9, 5]], "date-time": "2024-09-05T14:21:44Z", "timestamp": 1725546104183}, "edition-number": "0", "reference-count": 1, "publisher": "Psychology Press", "isbn-type": [{"type": "electronic", "value": "9780203337974"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2004, 11, 1]]}, "DOI": "10.4324/9780203337974-4", "type": "book-chapter", "created": {"date-parts": [[2021, 3, 6]], "date-time": "2021-03-06T06:39:25Z", "timestamp": 1615012765000}, "page": "13-20", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Evolving Conceptions of the Self and Memory"], "prefix": "10.4324", "member": "301", "reference": [{"key": "ref1", "unstructured": "Berkeley, G. (1734/1979). A treatise concerning the principles of human knowledge. In R. I. Watson (Ed.), Basic writings in the history of psychology. New York: Oxford University Press."}], "container-title": ["The Self and Memory"], "language": "en", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2021, 3, 6]], "date-time": "2021-03-06T06:39:27Z", "timestamp": 1615012767000}, "score": 27.39974, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/9781135432621/chapters/10.4324/9780203337974-4"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2004, 11, 1]]}, "ISBN": ["9780203337974"], "references-count": 1, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203337974-4", "published": {"date-parts": [[2004, 11, 1]]}}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T14:09:39Z", "timestamp": 1785334179708, "version": "3.55.0"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1767225600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/copyright-policy/copyright-basics"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Log anomaly detection is critical for ensuring the reliability of large-scale distributed systems. Existing methods often fail to balance adaptability to concept drift with the interpretability required for effective root cause analysis. Deep learning models generally lack transparency, while Large Language Model (LLM)-based approaches are prone to hallucination and exhibit high latency. To overcome these challenges, this paper proposes a neuro-symbolic framework, PGCLR+SESAM, which combines two complementary modules for robust log analysis. The first module, Prompt-Guided Causal Log Reasoning (PGCLR), uses an LLM (LLaMA-3.3-70B) and prompt-based causal reconstruction to infer multi-hop dependencies among log events and generate explicit root-cause reasoning chains. The second module, Self-Evolving Semantic Anomaly Memory (SESAM), implements an active memory-guided prompting mechanism. In contrast to passive storage, SESAM dynamically retrieves and injects semantic summaries of long-tail anomalies to refine the reasoning context and enhance generalisation under distribution drift. Extensive experiments on two large-scale datasets, LogHub~2.0 (15M entries) and LogLAB (9M entries), demonstrate that this approach outperforms previous methods, including specialized LLMs such as LLM-LADE, achieving an F1-score of 0.961, maintaining real-time inference efficiency with less than 150 ms per sequence. Automated interpretability metrics, including Graph Edit Distance and Semantic Consistency Score, as well as human evaluation, are used to quantitatively assess the structural accuracy of the generated explanations. The findings demonstrate that the proposed framework enables transparent causal reasoning and offers a practical solution for trustworthy, self-adaptive system reliability management.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.6818783", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T13:36:10Z", "timestamp": 1785332170000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Prompt-Guided Causal Log Reasoning with Self-Evolving Semantic Memory for Robust Anomaly Detection in Noisy Distributed Systems"], "prefix": "10.2139", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8426-0392", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Aming", "family": "Wu", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-8156-9569", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Jingzhe", "family": "Kang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Kaiming", "family": "Wang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-6212-0718", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Luyao", "family": "Liu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Wei", "family": "Zhao", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "78", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T13:36:10Z", "timestamp": 1785332170000}, "score": 27.039648, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=6818783"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6818783", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 1]], "date-time": "2026-07-01T12:56:36Z", "timestamp": 1782910596844, "version": "3.54.5"}, "publisher-location": "Stroudsburg, PA, USA", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Association for Computational Linguistics", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "DOI": "10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.917", "type": "proceedings-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 1]], "date-time": "2026-07-01T12:25:50Z", "timestamp": 1782908750000}, "page": "18423-18442", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["SEMA-RAG: A Self-Evolving Multi-Agent Retrieval-Augmented Generation Framework for Medical Reasoning"], "prefix": "10.18653", "author": [{"given": "Yongfeng", "family": "Huang", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Ruiying", "family": "Chen", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "James", "family": "Cheng", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "1643", "event": {"name": "Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026", "location": "San Diego, California, United States", "start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7]]}, "end": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7]]}}, "container-title": ["Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026"], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 1]], "date-time": "2026-07-01T12:41:48Z", "timestamp": 1782909708000}, "score": 26.912973, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.917"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.917", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 22]], "date-time": "2026-01-22T04:36:28Z", "timestamp": 17690